Write createBookCard(book). It should return an <li> that matches the example card in the HTML.
Write render(). It clears the list, then loops over books and appends one card for each book.

Hints:

Copy the example card’s structure into a comment and build it one element at a time from top to bottom: li → cover → body → title, author, meta, actions.
li.dataset.status = book.status sets the colour of the cover and the left edge. The CSS does the rest.
For the cover letter, think about how you get the first character of a string and make it uppercase.
Stars only show for finished books, so use an if. Look up String.prototype.repeat(): you need it twice, once for filled stars and once for empty ones.
Don’t write the three <option>s by hand. Make a small array like [{ value: "want", label: "Want to read" }, …] and forEach over it. You’ll reuse that array later for labels.
Set select.value = book.status after the options have been added.
Use textContent for everything the user typed, as you did in QuizMaster. A book called <b>Bold</b> should show exactly that text.
Leave the Edit and Delete buttons without listeners for now. Those come in step 5.

